dict(slug="solana-token-scam-check", date="2026-11-06", tag="Safety", tool=("Token Checkup", "/hq.html#checkup"),
 title="How to check if a Solana token is a scam (free, in 1 minute)",
 desc="A plain checklist of the public red flags behind most Solana rug pulls, and a free tool that checks them for any token address.",
 body=[
 ("quick", "Paste the token's mint address into a checker and look at five things: liquidity, pool age, mint and freeze authority, how concentrated the holders are, and whether people can actually sell. No check proves a token is safe, but these flags catch most obvious rugs."),
 ("p", "New Solana tokens appear every minute, and most of them lose almost all their value. You cannot read the minds of their creators, but you can read the public data. Here is what to look at, and why."),
 ("h2", "1. Liquidity: can anyone actually sell?"),
 ("p", "Liquidity is the money sitting in the trading pool. If a pool holds only a few thousand dollars, one medium sale can crash the price, and you may not be able to get out at a fair price. **Under $10,000 is a serious warning.** Under $50,000 still means big price swings."),
 ("h2", "2. Pool age"),
 ("p", "Most rug pulls happen in the first hours or days. A pool that is younger than one day has no track record at all. Waiting is free."),
 ("h2", "3. Mint and freeze authority"),
 ("ul", ["**Mint authority on:** someone can create new tokens at any time. New supply dilutes every holder.",
         "**Freeze authority on:** someone can freeze token accounts, so holders cannot move or sell.",
         "Both should be **revoked (off)**. This is one of the easiest checks and one of the most important."]),
 ("h2", "4. Who holds the tokens?"),
 ("p", "If the top 10 wallets hold most of the supply, or the wallet that created the token still holds a big share, a few people can dump on everyone else. Some of those wallets can be the pool itself or an exchange, so check them on Solscan before you panic."),
 ("h2", "5. Can people sell? (the honeypot test)"),
 ("p", "A honeypot lets you buy but not sell. A typical sign is many buys and not one single sell in the last 24 hours. Also be careful when the 24-hour volume is many times bigger than the liquidity: that can be bots trading with themselves to look busy."),
 ("h2", "Do it in one minute"),
 ("p", "Our free [Token Checkup](/hq.html#checkup) reads public GeckoTerminal data for any Solana token and lists every flag above in plain words, plus the launchpad status, 24-hour price spikes and more. No wallet, no account. Want to see what is trending right now with flags already added? Open the [MAIGA Radar](/radar.html)."),
 ("note", "A token with no flags is not safe; it only passed a few checks. Many risks cannot be seen in public data. This is education, not financial advice."),
 ("cta", ("Check a token now", "/hq.html#checkup")),
 ],
 faq=[("Can a tool prove a Solana token is safe?", "No. A checker only reads public data. It can show red flags, but a token without flags can still fail or be a scam."),
      ("What is the most important single check?", "Mint and freeze authority. If either is still on, the creator keeps powers that can hurt holders."),
      ("Is Token Checkup free?", "Yes. It runs in your browser, needs no wallet and no account.")]),

dict(slug="can-ai-predict-crypto-prices", date="2026-11-06", tag="AI", tool=("Kronos Lab", "/kronos.html"),
 title="Can AI predict crypto prices? We tested an open model 120 times",
 desc="We let the open-source Kronos model forecast 120 past moments of BTC, ETH, SOL, DOGE, XRP and PEPE. Here is the honest score.",
 body=[
 ("quick", "Not reliably. In our test of 120 past moments, the open-source Kronos model got the 24-hour direction right 68 times (57%). A coin flip gets 50%. And simply guessing \"the price stays the same\" was closer on average than the AI."),
 ("p", "Ads for \"AI trading bots\" promise a lot. We wanted a fair test you can check yourself, so we used a real research model and only forecast moments from the past, where we already know what happened."),
 ("h2", "How the test works"),
 ("ol", ["We take public hourly price data (no account, no keys) for six coins: BTC, ETH, SOL, DOGE, XRP and PEPE.",
         "We pick 20 moments per coin. The model sees the 360 hours before each moment and nothing after it.",
         "Kronos-small, an open model trained on charts from many exchanges, writes the next 24 hours, six times.",
         "We compare its middle guess with what really happened, and with a lazy \"no change\" guess."]),
 ("h2", "The result"),
 ("ul", ["**Direction right:** 68 of 120 moments (57%).",
         "**Average miss of the AI:** about 4.4% of the price after 24 hours.",
         "**Average miss of \"no change\":** about 2.9%. The lazy guess won.",
         "The numbers move a little every week, because a script runs the test again with fresh moments."]),
 ("h2", "Why this matters"),
 ("p", "57% sounds like an edge, but on 120 moments it can easily be luck, and trading fees eat small edges fast. Markets react to news that is not in any chart. Anyone selling a sure AI prediction is selling a story."),
 ("h2", "Try to beat the AI yourself"),
 ("p", "In the [Kronos Lab](/kronos.html) you see a real past chart, you guess higher or lower first, then the model guesses, then you see what really happened. Want to learn to read the chart yourself? Start with [Chart School](/chart.html)."),
 ("note", "Past moments only. No live forecasts, no signals, never about $MAIGA. Not financial advice."),
 ("cta", ("Open the Kronos Lab", "/kronos.html")),
 ],
 faq=[("Did the AI beat a coin flip?", "Slightly: 57% right on direction versus 50% for a coin flip. On only 120 moments that can be luck."),
      ("Which model was used?", "Kronos-small, an open-source model by Shi Yu and colleagues (MIT license), run on CPU in GitHub Actions."),
      ("Can I see the raw results?", "Yes. The Kronos Lab page shows every moment, the forecast band and the real price, per coin.")]),

dict(slug="what-is-a-honeypot-token", date="2026-11-06", tag="Safety", tool=("Token Checkup", "/hq.html#checkup"),
 title="What is a honeypot token, and how do you spot one?",
 desc="A honeypot lets you buy but never sell. Learn the warning signs in public data before you lose money.",
 body=[
 ("quick", "A honeypot token is built so that buying works but selling fails or is taxed to almost nothing. The clearest public sign is many buys and no sells. Check before you buy; afterwards it is too late."),
 ("h2", "How a honeypot works"),
 ("p", "The trick lives in the token's rules or the pool setup. The price chart only goes up, because nobody can sell. That rising chart is the bait. People buy, try to sell, and discover they cannot."),
 ("h2", "Warning signs you can see"),
 ("ul", ["**Buys but no sells** in the last 24 hours, for example 58 buys and 0 sells.",
         "**A flag from a data source:** GeckoTerminal marks some tokens as a possible honeypot.",
         "**Freeze authority still on:** the creator can freeze your account so you cannot move tokens.",
         "**Price only goes up** in a perfect line, often on a brand-new pool with little liquidity."]),
 ("h2", "What you can do"),
 ("ol", ["Paste the address into the free [Token Checkup](/hq.html#checkup) and read every flag.",
         "Look at the recent trades on an explorer: do you see real sells by different wallets?",
         "If you still want to try, never use money you cannot lose."]),
 ("note", "No public check catches every honeypot. Education only, not financial advice."),
 ("cta", ("Check a token", "/hq.html#checkup")),
 ],
 faq=[("Can I get my money back from a honeypot?", "Usually not. Blockchain transactions cannot be reversed. Prevention is the only real protection."),
      ("Does 'no honeypot flag' mean it is safe?", "No. It only means the data source saw no sign that selling is blocked.")]),

dict(slug="how-to-read-candlestick-charts", date="2026-11-06", tag="Learn", tool=("Chart School", "/chart.html"),
 title="How to read candlestick charts: a beginner's guide",
 desc="Candles, volume, moving averages and RSI explained in plain words, with free practice charts that use made-up data.",
 body=[
 ("quick", "Each candle shows four prices for one period: open, high, low and close. A green candle closed higher than it opened, a red one lower. Volume shows how much was traded. Learn the shapes on practice charts before you look at real money."),
 ("h2", "One candle, four prices"),
 ("ul", ["**Body:** the part between the open and the close.",
         "**Wicks:** the thin lines above and below, showing the highest and lowest price.",
         "**Colour:** green (or hollow) when it closed higher, red when it closed lower."]),
 ("h2", "Volume"),
 ("p", "The bars under the chart show how much was traded. A big move on tiny volume is easy to push and easy to reverse. A move with strong volume means more people took part."),
 ("h2", "Moving average"),
 ("p", "A moving average is the average close over the last few periods, drawn as a smooth line. It shows the general direction and filters out some noise. It always lags behind the price."),
 ("h2", "RSI"),
 ("p", "RSI is a number from 0 to 100 that compares recent gains with recent losses. Above 70 is often called overbought, under 30 oversold. It is a description of the past, not a prediction."),
 ("h2", "The pump-and-dump shape"),
 ("p", "A sudden vertical rise on thin volume, followed by a long red slide, is the classic pump-and-dump. Late buyers pay for the early sellers' profits."),
 ("h2", "Practise without risk"),
 ("p", "[Chart School](/chart.html) has seven short lessons and a \"guess the next candle\" game on made-up practice charts, so nothing on the page is a signal. Then test yourself against an AI in the [Kronos Lab](/kronos.html)."),
 ("cta", ("Open Chart School", "/chart.html")),
 ]),

dict(slug="how-does-a-blockchain-work", date="2026-11-06", tag="Learn", tool=("Mini-Chain", "/chain.html"),
 title="How does a blockchain work? Build a tiny one in your browser",
 desc="Hashes, blocks, mining and why you cannot change history, explained with a mini blockchain you can break yourself.",
 body=[
 ("quick", "A blockchain is a list of blocks where each block stores the fingerprint (hash) of the block before it. Change one letter in an old block and its fingerprint changes, which breaks every block after it. That is why history is hard to rewrite."),
 ("h2", "Step 1: the fingerprint"),
 ("p", "A hash function like SHA-256 turns any text into a fixed-length code. Change one letter and the code changes completely. You cannot work backwards from the code to the text."),
 ("h2", "Step 2: blocks linked by fingerprints"),
 ("p", "Each block holds some data, a number called the nonce, and the hash of the previous block. Because every block points to the one before, the blocks form a chain."),
 ("h2", "Step 3: mining"),
 ("p", "In proof-of-work chains like Bitcoin, a block only counts if its hash starts with a certain number of zeros. Miners try nonce after nonce until they find one. That takes work, and redoing it for many blocks takes even more."),
 ("h2", "Step 4: try to cheat"),
 ("p", "Edit an old block and you will see its hash change and every later block turn invalid. To fix it you must mine all of them again, faster than everyone else. On a big network that is practically impossible."),
 ("note", "Solana uses a different system (proof of stake with proof of history), but the hash-linked idea is the same."),
 ("p", "Open the [Mini-Chain](/chain.html), type anything, mine a block, then change one letter and watch the chain break. Nothing is sent anywhere and no real coins are involved."),
 ("cta", ("Open the Mini-Chain", "/chain.html")),
 ]),

dict(slug="is-solana-quantum-safe", date="2026-11-06", tag="Learn", tool=("Quantum Lab", "/quantum.html"),
 title="Is Solana safe from quantum computers? A plain explanation",
 desc="Why today's signatures could be broken by a large quantum computer one day, what post-quantum signatures are, and how to try them.",
 body=[
 ("quick", "Not on its own. Solana wallets sign with Ed25519, which a large enough quantum computer could break with Shor's algorithm. No such computer exists today. Post-quantum signatures like ML-DSA (a US standard since August 2024) are designed to resist that attack."),
 ("h2", "What a signature does"),
 ("p", "When you send coins, your wallet signs the transaction with your private key. Everyone can check the signature with your public key, but nobody can fake it without the private key."),
 ("h2", "The quantum threat"),
 ("p", "Shor's algorithm, run on a big and stable quantum computer, could work out a private key from a public key for Ed25519 and similar systems. Today's quantum machines are far too small and noisy for that. Nobody knows exactly when, or if, that changes."),
 ("h2", "Post-quantum signatures"),
 ("ul", ["**ML-DSA-65:** the official US standard (NIST FIPS 204, August 2024). Bigger keys and signatures: about 1,952 and 3,309 bytes.",
         "**Falcon-512:** smaller signatures, more complex to implement.",
         "**Ed25519 today:** 32-byte keys, 64-byte signatures. Small and fast, but not quantum-safe."]),
 ("h2", "What it means for $MAIGA"),
 ("p", "$MAIGA is a token on Solana, so it is exactly as quantum-safe as Solana itself. If Solana moves to post-quantum signatures, tokens on it follow."),
 ("p", "In the [Quantum Lab](/quantum.html) you can sign a message with Ed25519 and with the post-quantum algorithms, right in your browser, and compare sizes and speed."),
 ("cta", ("Open the Quantum Lab", "/quantum.html")),
 ],
 faq=[("Can a quantum computer steal crypto today?", "No. Current quantum computers are far too small to break Ed25519."),
      ("Is $MAIGA quantum-safe?", "Only as safe as Solana. It has no extra quantum protection of its own.")]),

